    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "a855650155187c4d364bf50df503007f",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1787,
    "chunk_text": "US marks 15 years of American Corner Jaffna; expands engagement with \u2018Freedom 250 Pavilion\u2019\n\nUS Embassy in Sri Lanka Public Affairs Officer Menaka Nayyar addressing the event marking the 15th anniversary of American Corner Jaffna\n\nUS Embassy in Sri Lanka Public Affairs Officer Menaka Nayyar (left) and Regional Public Engagement Specialist Miquela Burke at the anniversary event\n\nAttendees sign the \u2018Wishing Wall\u2019 at the Freedom 250 Pavilion, the new outdoor meeting area at American Corner Jaffna\n\nUS Embassy in Sri Lanka Public Affairs Officer Menaka Nayyar and Regional Public Engagement Specialist Miquela Burke alongside US Embassy Sri Lanka\u2019s Youth Forum Alumni\n\nThe US Embassy in Sri Lanka on 26 April celebrated the 15th anniversary of American Corner Jaffna (ACJ), highlighting its longstanding role in connecting communities in Northern Sri Lanka with the US through programs focused on education, innovation, and American values.\n\nThe Embassy also inaugurated the new \u2018Freedom 250 Pavilion,\u2019 part of the global Freedom 250 initiative, commemorating 250 years of American independence.\n\nEstablished in 2011 in partnership with the Jaffna Social Action Center, ACJ was created as a platform for direct engagement with local communities\u2014offering opportunities to learn about the US while building skills in critical thinking, leadership, and innovation. Launched in the post-conflict period, the Corner has played a key role in connecting emerging leaders in Northern Sri Lanka with ideas, resources, and opportunities rooted in American experience and perspectives.\n\nThe Freedom 250 Pavilion expands ACJ\u2019s capacity to host interactive programs and community events, reinforcing the Corner\u2019s role as a dynamic space for collaboration, dialogue, and learning."
  },
