    "chunk_text": "BALIFICATION: Interior designers in a dying garden\n\nIt is not merely a shift in aesthetic; it is a singularity. It is the rapid, ruthless homogenization of a landscape, engineered entirely to cater to a specific socio-economic group that worships one thing above all else: convenience.\n\nIt always starts under the innocent, glittering guise of \u201cprogress\u201d. A single, genuinely beautiful space opens up with soul and quality, like Citra or Trax. But in a paradise, a good thing never stays quiet for long. The whisper becomes a roar. The \u201cword is out\u201d.\n\nSuddenly, the battle cry of the modern colonizer echoes across the coast: \u201cSri Lanka is the next Bali! Ahangama is the new Canggu! Hiri is the next Uluwatu!\u201d. A friend of mine aptly calls this migratory circuit the \u201cRed Flag Triangle\u201d\u2014a perpetual loop between Bali, Portugal, and Sri Lanka. The red flags simply pack up their yoga mats and travel between them, demanding the exact same aesthetic wherever they land.\n\nWhat follows their arrival is an avalanche. A hundred lesser-quality versions of that first cafe spring up overnight. They serve the exact same imported slurry of avocado toast and perfectly poured flat whites. They are packed to the brim because the horde has arrived, and the horde demands the familiar. They want the wildness of the jungle, but only if it comes with high-speed Wi-Fi and cut-cement bathrooms. They want the aesthetic of the third world with the hyper-convenience of the first.\n\nBut what happens when you paste a Scandinavian-minimalist, Bauhaus-inspired lifestyle over a fragile, ancient ecosystem? You get a catastrophic disconnect."
