    "chunk_text": "What follows their arrival is an avalanche. A hundred lesser-quality versions of that first cafe spring up overnight. They serve the exact same imported slurry of avocado toast and perfectly poured flat whites. They are packed to the brim because the horde has arrived, and the horde demands the familiar. They want the wildness of the jungle, but only if it comes with high-speed Wi-Fi and cut-cement bathrooms. They want the aesthetic of the third world with the hyper-convenience of the first.\n\nBut what happens when you paste a Scandinavian-minimalist, Bauhaus-inspired lifestyle over a fragile, ancient ecosystem? You get a catastrophic disconnect.\n\nBut what is the colonizer\u2019s word for the niyagala mal (gloriosa superba), the glorious red flame lily? What is their word for the ancient  kumbuk trees, or the great hora trees whose massive canopies cast a sacred, life-giving shade over the rainforest that are the lungs of this planet?\n\nThere isn\u2019t one. Because they never cared to learn the architecture of the earth. And neither do the new colonizers.\n\nInstead of gathering under the deep, ancient shade of a  kumbuk tree in reverence, the new wave of expats\u2014cliques from various regions, operating like privileged fraternities\u2014gather in these new corroded metal bars to \u201cdrink the piss\u201d and get blind drunk, utterly oblivious to the unique and ancient land they are standing on.\n\nWe have suffered a collective loss of consciousness. We all know what a chair is. We know what a perfectly designed bar counter looks like. We are a society fluent in the language of interior design, obsessing over the sterile, curated arrangements of our indoor sanctuaries. But almost none of us know the plants that make up a garden."
  },
