    "chunk_text": "\u2018Just a Phase\u2019 The Forgotten Lunar Calendar\n\nThe International Centre for Ethnic Studies (ICES) will premiere its latest documentary, \u2018Just a Phase\u2019, on Friday, 15th May. The 59-minute documentary feature, directed by Sharni Jayawardena, will be screened at the PVR Cinema, One Galle Face, at 5 p.m.\n\nThe film explores an extraordinary yet largely forgotten episode in Sri Lanka\u2019s post-independence history. From January 1966 to September 1971, Ceylon replaced the Saturday\u2013Sunday weekend with the Pre-Poya and Poya days, which marked the four principal phases of the moon. This dramatic shift from the Gregorian to a lunar calendar was enacted through the Holidays Act No. 17 of 1965. The new calendar reshaped the rhythms of work, worship, and everyday life across the island.\n\nThrough archival research, interviews, and historical reflection, Just a Phase examines how a legislative act that may appear eccentric today was embedded in broader questions of nationhood, religion, and postcolonial identity. Indeed, the shift to a lunar calendar was shaped by the politics of post-independence Ceylon and tied to the restoration of traditions displaced under colonial rule, an effort closely associated with the Buddhist revivalist movement.\n\nIntroduced at a time when political parties were competing for the backing of influential Buddhist constituencies, the Act reflected the growing link between religion and electoral politics. Inevitably, the Holidays Act No. 17 also raised questions about whether it represented cultural revival, political expediency, or an attempt to redefine the state\u2019s identity."
