    "md5": "a9ba66eab099d281450978cf82f9c6e4",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1856,
    "chunk_text": "Merit, Integrity, and the future of Sri Lanka Cricket\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s cricketing journey has never been one of entitlement. It has been a story of disciplined progress, bold thinking, and earned success\u2014epitomised by the triumph at the 1996 Cricket World Cup under the leadership of Arjuna Ranatunga. That defining moment was not the product of sentiment or symbolism, but of clear-eyed selection, tactical clarity, and an unwavering commitment to merit.\n\nIt is precisely this foundation that must guide the game today.\n\nRecent discussions around governance reform have, in some quarters, been accompanied by speculative concerns about whether identity\u2014be it race, religion, or social background\u2014could influence national selection. While such ideas may not reflect formal policy, even their suggestion underscores the need to reaffirm a principle that has long underpinned successful cricketing nations: selection at the highest level must remain firmly rooted in performance, fitness, and role suitability.\n\nAcross the modern game, leading teams such as the Australia national cricket team, India national cricket team, and England cricket team offer a consistent lesson. Their strength lies not only in talent, but in systems that ensure credibility\u2014robust domestic competitions, transparent selection processes, and governance structures that minimise undue influence. These are not abstract ideals; they are practical standards aligned with the expectations of the International Cricket Council and the realities of elite sport.\n\nFor Sri Lanka, the path forward does not require reinvention, but recommitment.\n\nFirst, the credibility of selection must be beyond reproach. Independent panels, guided by clearly articulated criteria and supported by data and performance analytics, are essential to maintaining both fairness and public confidence."
  },
  {
